Z||000000||RobertVol||08-19-2000||12:34 PM||||First off the pick up coil.....<BR>ignition coil pickup coil differences?<P>also on this tach filter issue. I have heard it really isnt a big deal but since mine went out I have been having problems with a little bucking and when on the interstate it it has a miss the whole time consistently and the miss seems to happen when the tach is jumping. is it related? I have heard both ways. is there something that goes out when this goes out? <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Cliff Pennock||08-19-2000||02:59 PM||||Yes, they are related. Just for giggles, disconnect the tachfilter and see if you still have these problems.||||reg||1
Z||000002||RobertVol||08-19-2000||04:48 PM||||OK I will try that. Could the coil be adding to this? My mechanic says my coil is pushing 35000 does sound about right? I was thinking around 40000||||reg||1
Z||000003||mshill||08-20-2000||03:27 AM||||The coils could be replaced every 30,000 miles, but my 86SE had 185000 on it before it cut loose and took the module with it.  I am certain that the one on my 86GT has the original 95,000 miles on it as well.||||reg||1
Z||000004||RobertVol||08-20-2000||10:27 AM||||oops sorry I meant volts not miles  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||RobertVol||08-20-2000||11:17 AM||||But were in the heck is that Tach filter LOCATED? I cant seem to find it? I have bever had this problem with one of my cars. I want to disconnect and see how it  runs after that. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Old Lar||08-20-2000||07:51 PM||||The tach filter on the 2.8 V6 is just to the right of the distributor.  It is a small canister about the size of a BIC lighter.  It has a plug connection to it and is held in place by a clamp that attaches to the engine block.<P>I had to remove the heat shield around the distributor and take off the distributor cap to get at the clamp hold down nut.||||reg||3
Z||000007||2birds||08-20-2000||11:42 PM||||Mine used to buck/hiccup at highway speeds  too, and I had trouble getting it to start sometimes, until I replaced the pickup coil.  Original one fell apart as I removed it. New one was around $8 at Discount Auto, and took an hour to put in (I'm slow).  Don't forget the O-ring!!!||||reg||1
Z||000008||rprevo||08-21-2000||04:57 AM|||| Your ignition coil supplies the voltage<BR>to your rotor it's the big coil.<BR>Your pickup coil is underneath the rotor<BR>and ditributer removal is required for<BR>replacemeant, The only time I've ever had<BR>to change one is when the vacume advance<BR>moving back and fourth sawed one of the<BR>two wires that attach to the pickup coil.<BR>How ever when ever I replace my ignition<BR>coil for the first time I rebuild the <BR>distributer, New bushing, New module, New<BR>pickup coil. Whole ordeal about 50 bucks<BR>not counting your ignition coil. Then it's<BR>like new.<P>Ray<BR>||||reg||6
